  "avoidBefore": "lower",
  "energy": "A race lap lasts about 35-45 s: peak power comes in the first 2 s out of the gate and the rest is pumping and jumping, so it relies on the short-burst (ATP-PC) and glycolytic energy systems, with an aerobic base for repeat motos.",
  "summary": "BMX racing is decided by gate-start power and the strength to pump and absorb jumps; freestyle adds big landings and crashes. The plan builds heavy leg strength and power, upper-body and trunk strength for bike control, and shoulder, wrist and neck resilience for falls.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "Elite riders finish a race in about 40 s and spend only about a third of it pedalling; the rest is pumping and jumping, so BMX is a strength and power sport.",
      "source": "Cowell, Cronin & McGuigan 2011, Journal of Sports Science and Medicine",
      "url": "https://jssm.org/jssm-10-420.xml-Fulltext",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "In a systematic review, maximal strength (r = 0.84) and peak sprint power (r = 0.87) were strongly linked to BMX race performance, and jump height to race time.",
      "source": "Becerra-Patino et al. 2025, Journal of Functional Morphology and Kinesiology",
      "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12194740/",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Contusions, lacerations and fractures are the most common BMX injuries; injury rates reached 37.5 per 100 athletes at the 2016 Olympics, and prevention strategies are barely tested.",
      "source": "Scoping review 2025, Sports Health",
      "url": "https://lida.sport-iat.de/ta/Record/4090502?lng=de",
      "grade": "moderate"
    }
  ],
